@TRM
Feature: Term spelling — a term names the same pair however its author spelled it

  A target adopting this tool hand-types terms into two places: a check step's id: and a
  deferral tag. Every one of them is stored bare
  and looked up bare, because a hand writes a tag the way a feature file does — and every one of
  them was stripping the sigil BEFORE trimming, so a term padded in front of its @ was stored
  still carrying it and no bare lookup ever found it again. Nothing red. A check step cited
  nothing, a deferral tag deferred nothing — and each is a
  legitimate reading of a key the manifest never named, which is what made the miss silent
  instead of loud.

  IT NEVER FIRED HERE AND WOULD FIRE THERE, WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF ITS REACH. Every route is
  hand-typed and this repository generates none of them: a generated artefact carries no stray
  space. The consumer typing a deferral tag into their own package.json is the one who gets a
  tag that silently defers nothing, and they get it through the published binary.

  ONE RULE, SPELLED SEVEN TIMES, SO ONE BUG BECAME SEVEN. The correction is not the reordering;
  it is that a rule this small was hand-copied at all. dry's "a rule stated twice is a rule
  obeyed in its laxest form" with the laxest form being all of them. NOTHING MECHANICAL
  HOLDS THE COUNT AT ONE, AND IT IS SAID HERE RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE FOUND. No scan over this
  repository's own source is part of this Rule's evidence: its subject would be our tree rather
  than the product, so a target adopting this axis would inherit no such scan. An eighth
  hand-copy is one paste away and review is the whole of what
  catches it: an accepted residual, not an oversight.

  WHAT THIS DOES NOT CLAIM. It does not make the terms a sanitiser. A doubled sigil, an @ inside
  a term, whitespace inside a term, and case are each left exactly as they are and named here so
  the omissions are not silent: only a leading sigil is a sigil, and only whitespace AROUND a
  term is not part of it.

  Rule: a term names the same pair however its author spaced it around the sigil

    ONE TIER, AT EACH PORT. @unit proves the reading at each port that stores a term as a
    key: a configured deferral tag, a check step's id:, and each tag the Gherkin parser hands
    the corpus read. Each port is a pure function, so the padded, empty and doubled-sigil
    spellings are held where the term is read.

